#!/usr/bin/env bash

lord --verbose << "EOF"
Edit the source attributions in 01-genesis/49.tex so that they use the following fonts, as described in the prompt below:

Every time you switch fonts, add a comment fence (two comments) to the resulting latex file explaining why you switched fonts for a given interval.

Whenever you switch fonts, do this:

    \eXXX{...}
    \eYYY{...}

Never this:

    \eXXX{...\eYYY{...}...}

Work on this until you're satisfied with the result.

The prompt follows below:

---

For **Genesis 49 specifically**, I’d make **10th-century transitional Paleo-Hebrew your default ancient-poetry font**, and then visibly “age” later editorial material forward through your font chronology. I would **not** use Proto-Sinaitic, Ugaritic, or the 13th-century Proto-Canaanite fonts for the Blessing except as an explicitly speculative visual joke: even very early datings of the poem do not warrant a 15th–13th-century script. Izbet Sartah is the absolute earliest I would contemplate, and even that is probably too aggressive: its script is Early Alphabetic and the language of the inscription cannot securely be called Hebrew. ([Mnamon][1])

### My default: `08-bc10c-paleo-hebrew-tel-zayit.ttf`

That is almost absurdly well suited to what you are doing. Tel Zayit is **mid-10th century BCE**, and its letterforms are transitional: derived from the older Canaanite/Phoenician tradition but already anticipating the differentiated Hebrew national script. ([RCNi Company Limited][2])

So if the question is:

> “What might an early written edition of these old tribal poems have looked like around the formation of the monarchy?”

**Tel Zayit is probably your best font.**

I'd use your fonts something like this:

| Gen 49     | What I think you're representing              | Font I'd use                                                   | Why                                                                                         |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **2–7**    | very old tribal material: Reuben, Simeon/Levi | **`08-bc10c-paleo-hebrew-tel-zayit.ttf`**                      | Early material, but 10th c. is a much safer point for *writing it down* than 12th c.        |
| **8–9**    | old Judah saying, now tied to Judah's rise    | **Tel Zayit / Gezer**                                          | Transitional early-monarchic is perfect                                                     |
| **10**     | monarchic/dynastic Judah expansion            | **`09-bc09c-paleo-hebrew-standard-a.ttf`** or **Mesha-style**  | This line looks more politically developed than the underlying tribal sayings               |
| **11–12**  | Judah continuation; probably monarchic        | **Tel Zayit or 9th-c. standard**                               | Depends whether you treat 8–12 as one composition                                           |
| **13–17**  | Zebulun–Dan tribal sayings                    | **Tel Zayit / Gezer**                                          | Very plausible inherited early sayings                                                      |
| **18**     | “I wait for your salvation, YHWH”             | **`10-bc08c-paleo-hebrew-siloam-a.ttf`**                       | Best candidate for deliberately showing a conspicuous secondary insertion                   |
| **19–24a** | Gad–Joseph, old tribal/northern material      | **`08-bc10c-paleo-hebrew-gezer-a.ttf`**                        | Gezer feels especially good for the less-Judah-centric old material                         |
| **24b–26** | expanded Joseph blessing                      | **9th-c. Paleo-Hebrew**, perhaps `mesha-stele-a` aesthetically | These verses are a particularly plausible secondary expansion, though the date is uncertain |
| **27**     | Benjamin wolf saying                          | **Tel Zayit / Gezer**                                          | Sounds exactly like the old tribal-saying layer                                             |
| **28**     | prose/editorial colophon                      | **9th–8th-c. Paleo-Hebrew**                                    | Clearly frame/editor material rather than one of the sayings                                |

That division should be understood as a **critical visualization rather than a claim that paleography lets us date the verses**. Gen 49 is notoriously composite-looking. One major critical tradition sees separate tribal sayings collected and subsequently reworked; older criticism specifically flagged **v. 10, vv. 25–26, and probably v. 18** as interpolations. More recent work is more cautious and emphasizes substantial literary reworking rather than a clean recoverable sequence of interpolations. ([Jewish Encyclopedia][3])

## I would actually use **Gezer + Tel Zayit as two flavors of the oldest layer**

You have an unusually nice opportunity here.

`08-bc10c-paleo-hebrew-gezer-a.ttf`

for the **tribal-song material**, and

`08-bc10c-paleo-hebrew-tel-zayit.ttf`

for material that feels more specifically **Judah / early state scribal**.

The Gezer Calendar sits linguistically somewhere between Phoenician/Canaanite and Archaic Hebrew, perhaps even reflecting a dialect preceding the later dominance of Jerusalem Hebrew. ([Center for Online Judaic Studies][4]) Tel Zayit, meanwhile, comes from the Judaean Shephelah and is precisely a transitional early-Iron-II script heading toward distinctively Hebrew writing. ([RCNi Company Limited][2])

That makes the typography semantically useful rather than merely chronological:

**old pan-Israelite / tribal / dialectally weird stuff**
→ `gezer-a`

**early Judah / monarchy material**
→ `tel-zayit`

**clearly developed monarchic redaction**
→ `09-bc09c-paleo-hebrew-standard-a`

**8th-c. editorial hand**
→ `10-bc08c-paleo-hebrew-siloam-a`

**late First-Temple additions**
→ `11-bc07c-paleo-hebrew-ketef-hinnom-a`

**exilic / immediately pre-exilic**
→ `12-bc06c-paleo-hebrew-lachish-*.ttf`

**Persian-period editing**
→ `13-bc05c-imperial-aramaic-noto-sans.ttf`

That last transition matters: under Persian rule, Aramaic scribal practice became dominant, and by the fifth century BCE the Aramaic script was being standardized empire-wide. ([TheTorah][5]) So **Imperial Aramaic should mean something very strong in your book**: “I think this wording is Persian-period or later,” not merely “this is an editorial addition.”

### And that means I would *not* casually use Ketef Hinnom/Lachish either.

They are extremely useful chronological flags:

**Siloam** = 8th c. Hebrew. ([Wikipedia][6])
**Ketef Hinnom** = late 7th/early 6th c. Paleo-Hebrew. ([RCNi Company Limited][7])
**Lachish** = late 7th/early 6th c., right before Babylon. ([Biblical Archaeology Society][8])

So readers can eventually learn your typography almost unconsciously:

> weird angular old letters = **this shit is old**
> mature Paleo-Hebrew = **monarchy-era author/editor**
> elegant late Paleo-Hebrew = **late Judah**
> Aramaic-looking letters = **post-collapse scribal world**
> DSS square-ish hand = **really late textual history**

## For Gen 49, I'd therefore do this

```text
49:1       later prose frame              9th/8th c. Paleo-Hebrew
49:2-7     archaic collection             Gezer / Tel Zayit
49:8-9     early Judah material            Tel Zayit
49:10      monarchic expansion             9th-c. Paleo-Hebrew
49:11-17   old sayings                     Gezer / Tel Zayit
49:18      conspicuous insertion           Siloam
49:19-24a  old sayings / Joseph core       Gezer
49:24b-26  expanded Joseph blessing        9th-c. Paleo-Hebrew
49:27      old Benjamin saying             Gezer
49:28      compiler's colophon             9th/8th c. Paleo-Hebrew
```

I wouldn't pretend we can defend those century assignments verse-for-verse. The important historical point is that **the chapter does not belong cleanly to a single horizon**. Reuben and the non-priestly treatment of Levi can suggest an early setting, while Judah's political supremacy—especially 49:10—presupposes a very different situation. Encyclopaedia Judaica summarizes the problem nicely: the sayings resist assignment to one historical horizon, while the Judah material points toward the emergence of the monarchy/Davidic hegemony. ([Encyclopedia.com][9])

There is also a useful corrective from newer linguistic work: some supposedly “archaic” Torah poetry includes unmistakably Classical or even later Hebrew constructions. Gen 49 itself mixes old-looking material with forms that can fit exilic/post-exilic Hebrew, suggesting **old traditions that have been linguistically handled by later scribes**, rather than a pristine Iron-I poem frozen in amber. ([TheTorah][10])

That makes your font-switching idea **particularly appropriate**—provided you let the fonts represent *layers*, not simply assign one “date of Genesis 49.”

### One change I'd make to your catalog philosophy

I'd reserve

`07-bc12c-proto-canaanite-izbet-sartah-*`

for passages where you are making the unusually strong claim:

> **“This could plausibly descend from something already being recited/written in the Judges period.”**

Not merely “archaic Hebrew.”

And I would reserve

`06-bc13c-proto-canaanite-*`

for something even stronger.

The 15th/18th/19th-century Proto-Sinaitic files should basically be **off limits for ordinary biblical Hebrew composition**. They are fantastic for your Exodus alphabet-evolution business, but putting Gen 49 into them would visually imply a Bronze-Age textual antiquity that the evidence simply doesn't warrant.

**So if you want one answer:**

### `08-bc10c-paleo-hebrew-tel-zayit.ttf`

is the best single font for the Blessing of Jacob.

**If you want the maximally cool historically meaningful version:**

### Gezer/Tel Zayit → 9th-c. Paleo-Hebrew → Siloam

with the switches concentrated particularly around **49:10, 49:18, and 49:24b–26**.

And for **49:29–33**, if you're keeping your Hezekiah-era dating of P, `10-bc08c-paleo-hebrew-siloam-a.ttf` is almost comically perfect: actual 8th-century Judah Hebrew script for your P layer. ([Wikipedia][6])

